> erased? The scroll could be a counter-measure against an acute
> threat, say a horde of soldier ants, mordor orcs, or elf lords,
> but would not guarantee that you never see more of them.

What I was thinking was that if you're wiping out all of the
monsters of a type in the dungeon, that it really ought to
apply to the entire dungeon -- even the levels that haven't
actually been generated yet.  It seems unfair otherwise.

Maybe the rule could be that the monsters would not be created
during level generation, but could be otherwise generated --
the rationale being that the scroll of genocide wiped out
every monster of that type in the dungeon at the time, but
that other monsters generated came from elsewhere.

My original thought had been to initially set the random
generation probability to zero and allow it to gradually
increase back to its original level (kind of modeling monsters
migrating from wherever else they live into the dungeon over
time), but that seemed a little overcomplicated.

